+//! The closed segments a store is made of, and the one operation that changes them: a merge.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.8-compaction`. `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` gave the store a file worth
+//! keeping — records laid out by column, compressed, sealed — and said what it deliberately does
+//! not decide: *how many records one segment holds, and when a new one starts*. Both answers are
+//! this module's. A flush closes a segment ([`Segments::seal`]), and a store that only ever closed
+//! them would pay for every flush twice: a reader opens one more file per flush, and two segments
+//! holding the same series share nothing — not a dictionary, not a run, not a `zstd` frame. A merge
+//! is what fixes that ([`Segments::merge`]), and the decision of *which* segments it takes is
+//! [`plan`]'s, as data.
+//!
+//! # What the store is
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  <store>/ingest.log                 the log (`lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol` … `4.4-wal-recovery`)
+//!  <store>/segments/<n>.lks           one closed segment per file, named by its ordinal
+//!  <store>/segments.manifest          what the store is: an epoch and the segments it is made of
+//!  <store>/segments.manifest.pending  a manifest being written (see the swap, below)
+//! ```
+//!
+//! **The manifest is the store.** Not the directory listing, not the newest file: a segment counts
+//! exactly when the manifest names it, in the order it is listed, and everything else in
+//! `segments/` is an orphan a crash left behind. That is what makes a merge something a reader
+//! cannot catch half-done — the file set changes in one step, and the step is the manifest being
+//! replaced — and it is what makes recovering from a crash one rule instead of one rule per stage:
+//! *read the manifest, delete what it does not name*. A segment whose file is not the length the
+//! manifest states is refused rather than read, because an index that disagrees with its files is a
+//! store nobody can answer from: answering without that segment would answer a *hole*.
+//!
+//! # The five stages of a merge, and what a crash at each one leaves
+//!
+//! | stage | what is on the device | what the store is after a restart |
+//! | ----- | --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
+//! | `planned` | nothing: a plan is a value and no file was touched | the store it already was |
+//! | `written` | the new segment's file, in whatever part the device committed | the store it already was — the file is an orphan |
+//! | `durable` | the new segment, whole and flushed; the manifest does not name it | the store it already was — a whole orphan is still an orphan |
+//! | `swapped` | the manifest names the new set; the segments it replaced may still be there | the new set, and the leftovers go with the next open |
+//! | `retired` | the segments it replaced are gone | the new set |
+//!
+//! The stages between `planned` and `swapped` are [`Segments::merge`], the swap is
+//! [`Segments::install`], and `retired` is what a [`Snapshot`] does when the last reader that named
+//! the old set lets go. Every crash point above lands in one of two worlds — the old set or the new
+//! one — and never in between, which is the property this module is shaped around: **no accepted
+//! record is lost by a merge, and no reader sees it twice**.
+//!
+//! Two orderings make that true, and both are in the code rather than in a comment. The new segment
+//! is written *and flushed* before the manifest names it, so a crash cannot leave a store whose
+//! index points at bytes the device never kept; and the manifest is written to [`PENDING`] and
+//! renamed onto [`MANIFEST`], so a reader of the manifest sees the old one or the new one and never
+//! a half-written mix. A rename is atomic and a write is not, which is the whole reason the
+//! manifest is a whole file replaced rather than a file appended to.
+//!
+//! # What this module does not do
+//!
+//! A merge keeps every record it takes, in the order it found them: this store has no key that a
+//! later record could shadow, and nothing here deletes a record — `lakelet/4.9-retention` is where
+//! "remove" gets a second meaning, and it will have to say what happens to a record a merge is in
+//! the middle of copying. Nor does this module decide *when* a merge runs: [`Segments::compact`] is
+//! the whole job in one call for a background task, and `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` is what reads
+//! the segments a snapshot names when a query arrives.
+
+pub mod plan;
+pub mod snapshot;
+
+pub use plan::{Plan, Policy, SegmentInfo};
+pub use snapshot::Snapshot;
+
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+use std::sync::Arc;
+
+use crate::engine::segment;
+use crate::engine::wal::checksum;
+use crate::error::Error;
+use crate::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+use snapshot::Set;
+
+/// The directory inside the store directory that holds the closed segments.
+pub const DIRECTORY: &str = "segments";
+
+/// The file the store's segments are named in, at the top of the store directory.
+pub const MANIFEST: &str = "segments.manifest";
+
+/// The name a manifest is written under before it is renamed onto [`MANIFEST`].
+///
+/// A manifest is never edited in place: it is written whole here, flushed, and renamed. A crash
+/// therefore leaves either the manifest that was or the one being written, and the name below is
+/// the half-state a reader must never be handed — which is why an open deletes it.
+pub const PENDING: &str = "segments.manifest.pending";
+
+/// The extension a segment's file carries.
+pub const EXTENSION: &str = "lks";
+
+/// How many digits a segment's file name has, so a directory listing is in the manifest's order.
+const DIGITS: usize = 10;
+
+/// The file one segment's bytes live in: `<directory>/segments/<index>.lks`.
+///
+/// Public because the layout is part of what this store *is* — a repair tool, an operator with
+/// `ls`, and a test that has to damage a file all need to name one — and because the naming rule is
+/// what makes a directory listing readable in the order the manifest lists the segments.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn file_of(directory: &Path, index: u64) -> PathBuf {
+    directory.join(DIRECTORY).join(file_name(index))
+}
+
+/// What the store is: an epoch, and the segments it is made of, oldest first.
+///
+/// The epoch changes every time the store changes shape — a segment is closed, a merge is
+/// installed — and it is the number a [`Snapshot`] carries, so "these two readers saw the same
+/// store" is a comparison rather than an argument.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Manifest {
+    /// How many times the store has changed shape.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The segments, in the order their records were accepted.
+    segments: Vec<SegmentInfo>,
+}
+
+impl Manifest {
+    /// An empty store: no segments, and an epoch nothing has happened at yet.
+    fn empty() -> Self {
+        Self {
+            epoch: 0,
+            segments: Vec::new(),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// How many times the store has changed shape.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The segments that are the store, oldest first.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments(&self) -> &[SegmentInfo] {
+        &self.segments
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when the store holds no segments at all.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
+        self.segments.is_empty()
+    }
+
+    /// How many records the store holds, from the index alone.
+    ///
+    /// A number a caller asks for cheaply — how much a query will walk, how much memory an answer
+    /// might take — and the reason a segment's record count is written into the manifest when the
+    /// segment is closed: counting them any other way is reading every file this index exists to
+    /// avoid reading.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.segments
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, info| total.saturating_add(info.records))
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the store's segments cost together, from the index alone.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.segments
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, info| total.saturating_add(info.bytes))
+    }
+
+    /// This manifest as the bytes that go in the file.
+    ///
+    /// The layout is this module's own business — nothing outside the store reads the manifest — so
+    /// the only promises are the ones [`Manifest::decode`] keeps: whole or refused, and the same
+    /// segments back.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when this store holds so many segments that the count, or the body it
+    /// seals, does not fit the four bytes the layout gives it — a store no machine can have.
+    fn encode(&self) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
+        let _ = self;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// The manifest these bytes are, or a refusal naming what disagreed.
+    ///
+    /// Four comparisons and nothing clever: the magic and the version, the seal over the body, the
+    /// length the file has against the length the header states, and — after all of that — that the
+    /// bytes left are exactly the entries the count promises. A manifest a crash cut, or a device
+    /// changed, is a *refusal*: the alternative is reading it as a store with fewer segments, which
+    /// is a hole in every answer this store would give from then on.
+    fn decode(bytes: &[u8], path: &Path) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        let _ = (bytes, path);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+}
+
+/// The segments a store is made of, and what may change them.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub struct Segments {
+    /// The store directory: this module's files live at the top of it and below [`DIRECTORY`].
+    directory: PathBuf,
+    /// The rule a plan is made by.
+    policy: Policy,
+    /// The set the manifest names: what a [`Snapshot`] taken now holds.
+    current: Arc<Set>,
+    /// The ordinal the next segment gets — always past every index the manifest has ever named, so
+    /// a segment this store writes can never be confused with one a crash left behind.
+    next: u64,
+    /// The index of a merge that has been written and not installed yet, if there is one.
+    pending: Option<u64>,
+}
+
+impl Segments {
+    /// Open (or create) the segment store in `directory`, with the policy this build ships.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Store`] when the store or its segment directory cannot be created or read, and
+    /// [`Error::Protocol`] when the manifest is not this build's manifest or a segment it names is
+    /// not the length the manifest states.
+    pub fn open(directory: impl Into<PathBuf>) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        Self::with_policy(directory, Policy::DEFAULT)
+    }
+
+    /// Open (or create) the segment store in `directory`, with `policy` deciding what it merges.
+    ///
+    /// Opening is also what finishes the merge a crash interrupted, and it needs no case for which
+    /// stage that crash reached: the manifest names one set of segments, every file name in
+    /// `segments/` that the manifest does not name is an orphan, and an orphan is deleted. What the
+    /// manifest *does* name is checked against the files — a store with a segment whose bytes are
+    /// not the length the index states refuses to open, because the alternative is answering with a
+    /// hole in it.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Store`] when the directory cannot be created, read or flushed, and
+    /// [`Error::Protocol`] when the manifest or a segment it names is not what this build wrote.
+    pub fn with_policy(directory: impl Into<PathBuf>, policy: Policy) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        let _ = (directory, policy);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// The directory this store's segments live in.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn directory(&self) -> &Path {
+        &self.directory
+    }
+
+    /// The rule this store plans its merges by.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn policy(&self) -> Policy {
+        self.policy
+    }
+
+    /// What the store is: the epoch, and the segments it is made of.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn manifest(&self) -> &Manifest {
+        self.current.manifest()
+    }
+
+    /// What a reader holds: the segments that are the store right now, and the promise that they
+    /// are still there — and still this set — however many merges happen while the reader reads.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn snapshot(&self) -> Snapshot {
+        Snapshot::of(Arc::clone(&self.current))
+    }
+
+    /// Close `records` into a new segment, and make it part of the store.
+    ///
+    /// This is the call a flush ends with: the records a batch wrote are a segment, on the device,
+    /// named by the manifest in the position their records were accepted in. Like a merge it has an
+    /// order — the bytes are written and flushed *before* the manifest names them — so a crash
+    /// leaves either a store without the segment (and an orphan file) or a store with it, and never
+    /// a name pointing at bytes the device has not kept.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when `records` is empty — a segment holds records, and a store that made a
+    /// file out of nothing would carry it for ever — and everything the segment writer and the
+    /// manifest writer refuse.
+    pub fn seal(&mut self, records: &[Record]) -> Result<u64, Error> {
+        let _ = records;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// The merge this store's policy would do next, from the manifest alone.
+    ///
+    /// No side effect and no file read: the sizes and counts a plan is made from are what the
+    /// manifest already holds ([`Policy::plan`]).
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn plan(&self) -> Plan {
+        let _ = self;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// Write what `plan` asks for: read its segments in order, write them into one new segment, and
+    /// flush it.
+    ///
+    /// Nothing the store *is* changes here. The new file exists and is on the device, the manifest
+    /// does not name it, and a crash at this point leaves exactly that — a whole segment nobody
+    /// names, which the next open deletes. The reason the writing and the becoming are two calls is
+    /// that a merge takes as long as it takes: [`Segments::merge`] is the work, and
+    /// [`Segments::install`] is the one step a reader can be in the middle of.
+    ///
+    /// One merge at a time: a `Merge` names a file that is not the store yet, and a second merge
+    /// would write over it.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when the plan is empty, names a segment the manifest does not have, or
+    /// names segments that are no longer one run — a plan is a value taken at a moment, and a store
+    /// that carried out a stale one would write a segment holding records out of order. Everything
+    /// the segment reader, the segment writer and the device refuse is an [`Error::Store`].
+    pub fn merge(&mut self, plan: &Plan) -> Result<Merge, Error> {
+        let _ = plan;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// Make the merge `ran` the store: one manifest, written whole and renamed into place.
+    ///
+    /// This is the step a reader cannot be in the middle of, and it is one call because it is one
+    /// thing: the manifest naming the new set replaces the manifest that named the old one. The
+    /// segments it replaced are *retired* rather than deleted — the last [`Snapshot`] that named
+    /// them deletes them — which is what lets a read that began before this call answer the same
+    /// thing as one that ran entirely before it.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when this store has no merge in flight at `ran`'s index, and
+    /// [`Error::Store`] when the manifest cannot be written or put in place.
+    pub fn install(&mut self, ran: Merge) -> Result<MergeReport, Error> {
+        let _ = ran;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// The whole job in one call — plan, merge, install — for the caller that has nothing to decide:
+    /// a background task, a test, a repair tool.
+    ///
+    /// `None` means the policy has nothing left to merge, which is the answer such a caller loops on
+    /// until a flush gives it something again. `Some(report)` is what one merge cost.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// Everything [`Segments::merge`] and [`Segments::install`] refuse.
+    pub fn compact(&mut self) -> Result<Option<MergeReport>, Error> {
+        let _ = self;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+
+    /// Put a manifest in place: written to [`PENDING`], flushed, renamed onto [`MANIFEST`], and the
+    /// directory flushed after the rename.
+    ///
+    /// Three writes, in this order, and the order is the crash point: the bytes, then the name that
+    /// makes them the store, then the directory entry that keeps the name. A crash before the rename
+    /// leaves the manifest that was — the file below is an orphan the next open deletes — and a
+    /// crash after it leaves the new one, so a reader of the manifest is never handed a
+    /// half-written index.
+    fn write_manifest(&self, manifest: Manifest) -> Result<(), Error> {
+        let _ = manifest;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+}
+
+/// A merge that has been written and made durable, and is not the store yet.
+///
+/// The value [`Segments::merge`] answers with and [`Segments::install`] takes: the stage between
+/// "the new segment is on the device" and "the store is the new set". Holding it is holding a file
+/// nobody names, which is why it is `#[must_use]`: installing it makes the store the new set, and
+/// dropping it leaves an orphan for the next open to delete.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+#[must_use = "a merge that is never installed leaves a segment nobody names: install it, or the next open deletes it"]
+pub struct Merge {
+    /// The epoch the store would have if this merge were installed.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The new segment's ordinal.
+    index: u64,
+    /// Its file.
+    file: PathBuf,
+    /// The segments it replaces, oldest first.
+    taken: Vec<u64>,
+    /// How many bytes its file holds.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// How many bytes the segments it replaces hold together.
+    bytes_in: u64,
+    /// How many records it holds.
+    records: u64,
+    /// How many segments the store was made of when this merge was planned.
+    segments_before: usize,
+}
+
+impl Merge {
+    /// The epoch the store would have if this merge were installed.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The new segment's ordinal — the index it has in the manifest once installed.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn index(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.index
+    }
+
+    /// The file the new segment was written to.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn file(&self) -> &Path {
+        &self.file
+    }
+
+    /// The segments this merge replaces, oldest first.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn taken(&self) -> &[u64] {
+        &self.taken
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes its file holds.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the segments it replaces hold together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes_in(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes_in
+    }
+
+    /// How many records it holds.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one merge cost and what it changed, in counts.
+///
+/// The report a store's own bookkeeping is written in: how many files it took, how many bytes went
+/// in and came out, how many records came through. A merge that rewrote the store over and over
+/// would show up here as a [`MergeReport::bytes_removed`] of nothing against a
+/// [`MergeReport::bytes_out`] of everything, which is what `tests/budget_compaction.rs` measures and
+/// the step's page argues about.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct MergeReport {
+    /// The epoch the store moved to.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The segment the merge wrote.
+    wrote: u64,
+    /// The segments it replaced.
+    merged: Vec<u64>,
+    /// What those segments cost.
+    bytes_in: u64,
+    /// What the segment that replaced them costs.
+    bytes_out: u64,
+    /// How many records came through.
+    records: u64,
+    /// How many segments the store was made of.
+    segments_before: usize,
+    /// How many it is made of now.
+    segments_after: usize,
+}
+
+impl MergeReport {
+    /// The epoch the store moved to.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The segment the merge wrote — its ordinal, which is the index it has in the manifest.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn wrote(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.wrote
+    }
+
+    /// The segments it replaced, oldest first.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn merged(&self) -> &[u64] {
+        &self.merged
+    }
+
+    /// What those segments cost together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes_in(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes_in
+    }
+
+    /// What the segment that replaced them costs.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes_out(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes_out
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the store stopped holding: what went in, less what came out.
+    ///
+    /// Zero when a merge wrote back at least as much as it took — which is not a failure of the
+    /// merge (every record is still there) and is exactly what this number is for: it is the half of
+    /// write amplification a policy decides, and the budget lane reads it against
+    /// [`MergeReport::bytes_out`].
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes_removed(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes_in.saturating_sub(self.bytes_out)
+    }
+
+    /// How many records came through.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+
+    /// How many segments the store was made of before the merge.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments_before(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments_before
+    }
+
+    /// How many it is made of after it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments_after(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments_after
+    }
+
+    /// How many files the store stopped keeping: the count a reader pays for, moved.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn files_removed(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments_before.saturating_sub(self.segments_after)
+    }
+}
+
+/// The name a segment's file has.
+fn file_name(index: u64) -> String {
+    format!("{index:0DIGITS$}.{EXTENSION}")
+}
