@@ -0,0 +1,190 @@
+//! The merge policy as a value: *which* segments a merge would take, decided from their sizes and
+//! counts, touching no file at all.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.8-compaction`, and the half of it that needs no disk. Every flush closes a
+//! segment (`lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments`), so a store that only ever appends them pays for
+//! every flush twice: a reader opens one more file when it answers, and the columns of two
+//! segments that hold the same series cannot share anything — not a dictionary, not a run, not a
+//! `zstd` frame. What fixes that is a merge, and *which* segments a merge takes is a decision with
+//! a price on both sides: merging too eagerly rewrites bytes that were already written once, and
+//! merging too lazily leaves a reader opening a hundred files to answer one query. This module is
+//! that decision as data — [`Policy`] and a list of [`SegmentInfo`] in, a [`Plan`] out — so that
+//! what the store *would* do can be judged without a store and without a side effect.
+//!
+//! # The rule this build ships
+//!
+//! **Size tiering**, in one sentence: *a plan takes the oldest run of `width` consecutive segments
+//! whose sizes are all within a factor of `ratio` of each other.* Four words in it carry the whole
+//! design, and each one is a thing a policy can get wrong:
+//!
+//! * **sizes, not records** — the cost of a merge is bytes read and bytes written, and the cost of
+//!   *not* merging is files a reader opens; a segment's record count is what a plan reports so that
+//!   a caller can say what the merge is about, but the decision is made from bytes;
+//! * **comparable, both ways** — a run extends while the next segment's size is within `ratio` of
+//!   the run's smallest *and* of its largest. One direction is not enough: with a segment ten times
+//!   the size of its neighbours, a rule that only compared the newcomer with the smallest would
+//!   keep extending the run and merge a small file into a large one, which is the rewrite this
+//!   whole design is trying to avoid;
+//! * **consecutive** — the manifest's order is the order the records were accepted in, and a merge
+//!   writes one segment where its inputs were. A run that skipped a segment would move that
+//!   segment's records behind records that came after them, so a plan is a contiguous run or it is
+//!   not a plan;
+//! * **oldest** — of the runs that qualify, the plan takes the one that starts first. Those
+//!   segments were written first, they are the ones a reader has been opening longest, and a
+//!   policy that picked the *youngest* run would leave the old ones to grow old forever.
+//!
+//! A plan takes exactly `width` segments, never a whole tier: a merge whose cost followed the size
+//! of the tier would depend on how long the store had been running, and a store that pays an
+//! unbounded cost because it has been up for a month is a store that stalls. A merge of one
+//! segment is not a plan either — it rewrites bytes and removes none.
+
+/// One segment as a plan sees it: what it is called, how many bytes it costs, what it holds.
+///
+/// This is the manifest's view of a segment (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`), which is why a plan can be
+/// made without opening a single file: the size and the record count are what the store wrote down
+/// when it closed the segment, and a decision that had to read every file it is deciding about
+/// would pay the cost it is trying to avoid.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct SegmentInfo {
+    /// The segment's ordinal. Its file is this number inside the store's segment directory, and the
+    /// manifest lists segments by it in the order their records were accepted.
+    pub index: u64,
+    /// How many bytes its file holds.
+    pub bytes: u64,
+    /// How many records it holds.
+    pub records: u64,
+}
+
+impl SegmentInfo {
+    /// One segment, as the store writes it into the manifest.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(index: u64, bytes: u64, records: u64) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            index,
+            bytes,
+            records,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The rule this store merges by: `width` segments whose sizes are within `ratio` of each other.
+///
+/// A policy is data on purpose. The numbers are a decision somebody has to defend — this build's
+/// are in [`Policy::DEFAULT`] and in the step's page, and `tests/budget_compaction.rs` measures what
+/// they answer on a store of comparable segments — and the rule around them is
+/// [`Policy::plan`]'s, which is the part every policy shares.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Policy {
+    /// How many segments one merge takes.
+    width: usize,
+    /// How far two segments' sizes may be apart, as a factor, and still be one merge's inputs.
+    ratio: u64,
+}
+
+impl Policy {
+    /// The policy this build ships: four segments whose sizes are within a factor of two.
+    ///
+    /// Both numbers were measured rather than picked, and `tests/budget_compaction.rs` prints the
+    /// numbers they answer on sixteen comparable segments (five merges, one file left, and the
+    /// bytes rewritten per byte removed in `budget/4.8-compaction.toml`). The two directions of
+    /// getting them wrong are a bound and a file count: merging two segments at a time lowers the
+    /// file count fastest and rewrites the store about four times for every byte it removes, and a
+    /// width of eight spends a store's whole read bandwidth on one call and leaves the last three
+    /// segments of a store that only ever holds seven to grow old unmerged. A factor of two is what
+    /// makes the merged segment leave its tier — four equal segments of `s` bytes became about
+    /// `3.2 s`, which is not comparable with `s` — so the next merge happens in the next tier
+    /// instead of rewriting what the last one wrote.
+    pub const DEFAULT: Policy = Policy { width: 4, ratio: 2 };
+
+    /// A policy with these numbers.
+    ///
+    /// A `width` below two is treated as two when a plan is made (`Policy::plan`): rewriting one
+    /// file into one file costs bytes and removes none.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(width: usize, ratio: u64) -> Self {
+        Self { width, ratio }
+    }
+
+    /// How many segments one merge takes.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn width(&self) -> usize {
+        self.width
+    }
+
+    /// How far apart two segments' sizes may be and still be one merge's inputs.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn ratio(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.ratio
+    }
+
+    /// The merge this policy would do next: the oldest run of [`Policy::width`] consecutive segments
+    /// whose sizes are all within [`Policy::ratio`] of each other.
+    ///
+    /// The answer is data and this call has no side effect: it may be asked twice, asked about a
+    /// store that is being merged by somebody else, or asked by a test that has no store at all. A
+    /// plan that names segments the store no longer has is refused at the moment it is carried out
+    /// (`Segments::merge`) rather than repaired here, because a planner cannot promise what the
+    /// store looks like when the merge starts.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn plan(&self, segments: &[SegmentInfo]) -> Plan {
+        let _ = (self, segments);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.8-compaction")
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one merge would take: a contiguous run of segments, oldest first, and what they cost.
+///
+/// The plan is a value a caller can hold, log, or refuse. Its segments are indices into the
+/// manifest's order, and they are ascending because the run it describes is contiguous — the
+/// property that lets a merge write one segment without moving a record in front of another.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Plan {
+    /// The segments the merge would take, in the manifest's order.
+    segments: Vec<u64>,
+    /// What those segments hold, added up.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// How many records that is.
+    records: u64,
+}
+
+impl Plan {
+    /// No merge: there is no run of this policy's shape in the store.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn empty() -> Self {
+        Self {
+            segments: Vec::new(),
+            bytes: 0,
+            records: 0,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when this plan is no merge at all.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
+        self.segments.is_empty()
+    }
+
+    /// The segments the merge would take, in the manifest's order.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments(&self) -> &[u64] {
+        &self.segments
+    }
+
+    /// How many segments that is.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments.len()
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes those segments cost together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// How many records they hold together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+}
