--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/sim_compaction.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,843 @@
+//! The merge schedules `lakelet/4.8-compaction` is judged by: readers that hold a snapshot across a
+//! merge, and five ways for a store to die in the middle of one.
+//!
+//! Course content — the step's scaffold ships it, `slow:sim@N` runs it once per seed with
+//! `KILN_SIM_SEED` set, and the gate compares the `lakelet-sim` lines below in the order they were
+//! printed. Two things are therefore strict:
+//!
+//! * **the transcript is the schedule.** Every line is an event this scenario observed, and the seed
+//!   decides the order they happen in (`turmoil::Builder::rng_seed`, plus `enable_random_order` so
+//!   the nodes are stepped in a random order too). Which reader is inside a read when a merge starts,
+//!   and which of them asks next, is exactly the answer that depends on that order — and a merge
+//!   beside a reader is what this step is about. Nothing here prints a wall clock, a path, an
+//!   address or a record's own value: the digests are of the records, the numbers are counts;
+//! * **the invariants are asserted here, not by the gate.** The gate can only see whether a run
+//!   passed. Three claims are checked inside the scenario, and a schedule that breaks one fails the
+//!   run with the sentence that says which:
+//!   1. **every acknowledged record is answered exactly once, in order.** The notebook is what this
+//!      harness sealed; a reader's records are compared with its prefix, record for record, and a
+//!      merge that lost or duplicated one fails the run that saw it;
+//!   2. **a reader that began before the swap answers what a reader that ran entirely before it
+//!      answered.** A reader holds its snapshot across the merge, and the digest of its answer is
+//!      compared with the digest of the last reader that named the same epoch;
+//!   3. **a crash at each stage of a merge leaves one of the two worlds.** After a death the store is
+//!      reopened and has to be the set it was or the set the merge would make, with the notebook
+//!      intact and the directory holding exactly the segments the manifest names.
+//!
+//! # What the five deaths model
+//!
+//! A store here is a `Segments` — the real files, the real manifest, real flushes — plus this
+//! harness's notebook of what it sealed. Only the notebook outlives a death, the way a test's notes
+//! outlive the process they are about, and every death is modelled by *leaking* the store instead of
+//! dropping it: a process death runs no destructors, so the files a merge had retired stay on the
+//! disk for the next open to find. That is the one thing a model like this can get wrong, and it is
+//! the difference between a crash and a clean stop.
+//!
+//! Nothing here puts a manifest back either. What a death takes is the part of a write the device
+//! never committed — so the three stages before the swap are modelled by simply not installing, and
+//! a merge that made itself visible while it wrote its segment dies here rather than being tidied up
+//! by this harness. The `world=` field of the `restart` line is where that shows: it is checked
+//! against the stage that killed the store, not against what the code chose to do.
+//!
+//! * **`planned`** (`crash planned`): the plan was made and nothing else happened. A plan is a
+//!   value; the store on the disk is the store it was;
+//! * **`written`** (`crash written`): the process died while the new segment was being written, so
+//!   the file is there in the part the device committed and the manifest does not name it. A device
+//!   that commits part of a write is what a write cache is, and this is `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s
+//!   torn record one level up — except that a segment is *whole or refused*, so this file is never
+//!   read at all: it is an orphan;
+//! * **`durable`** (`crash durable`): the new segment is whole and flushed, and the process died
+//!   before the manifest was replaced. A whole segment nobody names is still nobody's segment;
+//! * **`swapped`** (`crash swapped`): the manifest names the new set and the segments it replaced
+//!   are still on the disk, because a reader was still holding them when the process died. The store
+//!   is the *new* set — there is no third world — and the leftovers are cleaned by the next open;
+//! * **`retired`** (`crash retired`): the merge is complete, and the old segments were deleted
+//!   before the death. The store is the new set with nothing left over.
+//!
+//! # Why the seed is not in the transcript
+//!
+//! `slow:sim@N` demands that at least two seeds produce different transcripts — three identical ones
+//! would mean the suite never read the seed — so the seed is deliberately kept out of every line:
+//! what tells the seeds apart is the order of the lines, not a number spell out in the first one.
+//!
+//! # The lines the gate reads
+//!
+//! * `sealed` — a flush closed a segment, with what it holds and where the store is now;
+//! * `begin` / `read` — a reader took a snapshot, and what it answered from it; `same` when the
+//!   epoch it named had already been answered by another reader, with the digest that has to match;
+//! * `merge` — one merge, in counts: what it took, what it wrote, what the store stopped holding,
+//!   and how many files are on the disk while a reader still names the old set;
+//! * `crash` / `restart` — the stage a store died at, and the world it came back as;
+//! * `verify` / `done` — the store's own account at the end: every record once, in order.
+
+use std::collections::HashMap;
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+use std::sync::Arc;
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+use tokio::io::{AsyncBufReadExt, AsyncWriteExt, BufReader};
+use tokio::sync::Mutex;
+use turmoil::Builder;
+use turmoil::net::{TcpListener, TcpStream};
+
+use lakelet::engine::compaction::{DIRECTORY, Segments, Snapshot};
+use lakelet::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+/// The seed this run uses when the judge does not name one.
+const DEFAULT_SEED: u64 = 1;
+
+/// The variable `slow:sim@N` sets: which schedule this run takes.
+const SEED: &str = "KILN_SIM_SEED";
+
+/// The store's name and port in the simulated network.
+const HOST: &str = "store";
+const PORT: u16 = 7000;
+
+/// Records one sealed segment holds.
+const PER_SEGMENT: usize = 32;
+
+/// Series the records are spread over.
+const SERIES: usize = 4;
+
+/// Segments the store is built from before the readers start.
+const SEGMENTS: usize = 8;
+
+/// Segments each crash phase seals before it interrupts a merge.
+const MORE: usize = 4;
+
+/// Readers racing the merges.
+const READERS: [&str; 2] = ["spanning", "steady"];
+
+/// Rounds each reader reads.
+const ROUNDS: usize = 3;
+
+/// How long a client waits between two polls of the host's status, in simulated milliseconds.
+const POLL: Duration = Duration::from_millis(10);
+
+/// The stages of a merge this scenario kills a store at, in the order it walks them.
+const STAGES: [&str; 5] = ["planned", "written", "durable", "swapped", "retired"];
+
+/// A failure inside the scenario: the one sentence the run reports.
+type Failure = Box<dyn std::error::Error>;
+
+/// The seed this process was asked for.
+fn seed() -> u64 {
+    match std::env::var(SEED) {
+        Ok(text) => text
+            .parse()
+            .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("{SEED}=`{text}` is not a number: {error}")),
+        Err(_) => DEFAULT_SEED,
+    }
+}
+
+/// The records one sealed segment holds, decided by the two numbers that name them: the judge can
+/// rebuild every record the store was given without reading the store it is judging.
+fn records_for(index: usize) -> Vec<Record> {
+    let first = index * PER_SEGMENT;
+    (0..PER_SEGMENT)
+        .map(|offset| Record {
+            series: format!("sim,host=h{},seg={index}", (first + offset) % SERIES),
+            ts_millis: 1_700_000_000_000 + (first + offset) as i64 * 1_000,
+            value: ((first + offset) % 1_024) as f64 / 8.0,
+        })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// A digest of a record list: the value compared by bits, so `-0.0` and `NaN` are the values they
+/// are and not whatever a comparison says.
+///
+/// One number rather than the records themselves, because a transcript is compared between runs and
+/// a schedule's digest is what makes two reads comparable; the algorithm is FNV-1a, written out so
+/// that this file depends on nothing but the standard library.
+fn digest(records: &[Record]) -> u64 {
+    records
+        .iter()
+        .fold(0xcbf2_9ce4_8422_2325u64, |hash, record| {
+            let hash = mix(hash, record.series.as_bytes());
+            let hash = mix(hash, &[0]);
+            let hash = mix(hash, &record.ts_millis.to_le_bytes());
+            mix(hash, &record.value.to_bits().to_le_bytes())
+        })
+}
+
+/// One FNV-1a step: `bytes` folded into `hash`.
+fn mix(hash: u64, bytes: &[u8]) -> u64 {
+    let mut hash = hash;
+    for byte in bytes {
+        hash ^= u64::from(*byte);
+        hash = hash.wrapping_mul(0x0000_0100_0000_01b3);
+    }
+    hash
+}
+
+/// `true` when every record is the same record, in the same order.
+fn same(left: &[Record], right: &[Record]) -> bool {
+    left.len() == right.len()
+        && left.iter().zip(right).all(|(left, right)| {
+            left.series == right.series
+                && left.ts_millis == right.ts_millis
+                && left.value.to_bits() == right.value.to_bits()
+        })
+}
+
+/// One whitespace-separated command word, as a number.
+fn number(word: Option<&str>, what: &str) -> Result<usize, Failure> {
+    let Some(word) = word else {
+        return Err(format!("this command needs a {what}").into());
+    };
+    word.parse()
+        .map_err(|error| format!("`{word}` is not a {what}: {error}").into())
+}
+
+/// A list of segment ordinals, as a transcript line spells it.
+fn list(indices: &[u64]) -> String {
+    indices
+        .iter()
+        .map(u64::to_string)
+        .collect::<Vec<String>>()
+        .join(",")
+}
+
+/// How many files the store's segment directory holds.
+fn files_in(directory: &Path) -> u64 {
+    std::fs::read_dir(directory.join(DIRECTORY))
+        .map(|entries| entries.filter_map(std::result::Result::ok).count() as u64)
+        .unwrap_or(0)
+}
+
+/// One reader's held snapshot, and how many records it named when it took it.
+struct Held {
+    /// What the reader is reading.
+    snapshot: Snapshot,
+    /// How many records its manifest said the store held.
+    records: u64,
+}
+
+/// The store the scenario drives: the real segment store, the notebook of what it was given, and
+/// what the readers are holding.
+struct Host {
+    /// The store directory, which outlives every store (and every death) in this scenario.
+    directory: PathBuf,
+    /// The store, or `None` between a death and the restart after it.
+    store: Option<Segments>,
+    /// Every record that was sealed, in the order it was sealed: what the store owes a reader.
+    notebook: Vec<Record>,
+    /// How many segments have been sealed, which is the ordinal the next one gets.
+    sealed: u64,
+    /// What each reader took and has not let go of.
+    held: HashMap<String, Held>,
+    /// The digest each epoch has been answered with, so two readers of one store are comparable.
+    answers: HashMap<u64, u64>,
+    /// The stage of the last death, for the restart to name.
+    stage: String,
+    /// The epoch the store has after that death, which is one of the two worlds.
+    world: u64,
+    /// Which of the two worlds that epoch is: the set of segments the store had, or the one the
+    /// merge would have made.
+    world_name: &'static str,
+    /// How many merges happened.
+    merges: u64,
+    /// How many deaths happened.
+    deaths: u64,
+    /// How many reads answered.
+    reads: u64,
+    /// How many readers have said they are finished.
+    finished: usize,
+    /// How many clients have connected, the controller included.
+    clients: u64,
+    /// Which merge the readers wait for before finishing a crossing read.
+    phase: u64,
+    /// A failure somewhere in the host, which every later command reports.
+    failed: Option<String>,
+}
+
+impl Host {
+    /// A host with an empty store and an empty notebook.
+    fn open(directory: PathBuf) -> Result<Self, Failure> {
+        let store = Segments::open(&directory)?;
+        Ok(Self {
+            directory,
+            store: Some(store),
+            notebook: Vec::new(),
+            sealed: 0,
+            held: HashMap::new(),
+            answers: HashMap::new(),
+            stage: String::new(),
+            world: 0,
+            world_name: "old",
+            merges: 0,
+            deaths: 0,
+            reads: 0,
+            finished: 0,
+            clients: 0,
+            phase: 0,
+            failed: None,
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// The store, or the failure that says why there is none.
+    fn live(&mut self) -> Result<&mut Segments, Failure> {
+        match self.store.as_mut() {
+            Some(store) => Ok(store),
+            None => Err(
+                "the store is down: this command arrived after a death and before the \
+                         restart that brings it back"
+                    .into(),
+            ),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// What the store answers right now, checked against the notebook.
+    ///
+    /// The whole of the first invariant, in one call: a reader of the store gets every record that
+    /// was sealed, once, in the order it was sealed. A merge that dropped one, wrote one twice or
+    /// put one behind another fails here with the counts in the sentence.
+    fn answers_the_notebook(&mut self) -> Result<u64, Failure> {
+        let store = self.live()?;
+        let read = store.snapshot().records()?;
+        if !same(&read, &self.notebook) {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "the store answered {} record(s) where {} were sealed: every acknowledged record \
+                 appears exactly once across what a snapshot names, in the order the store \
+                 accepted it — and a merge is the one thing that can put a record behind another, \
+                 hand it back twice, or drop it",
+                read.len(),
+                self.notebook.len()
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        Ok(read.len() as u64)
+    }
+
+    /// Seal `count` more segments, and say what the store is afterwards.
+    fn seal(&mut self, count: usize) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        let mut sealed = Vec::new();
+        for _ in 0..count {
+            let index = self.sealed;
+            let records = records_for(index as usize);
+            let store = self.live()?;
+            store.seal(&records)?;
+            self.notebook.extend(records);
+            self.sealed += 1;
+            sealed.push(index);
+            let (epoch, segments, bytes) = {
+                let store = self.live()?;
+                let manifest = store.manifest();
+                (
+                    manifest.epoch(),
+                    manifest.segments().len(),
+                    manifest.bytes(),
+                )
+            };
+            println!(
+                "lakelet-sim sealed segment={index} records={PER_SEGMENT} bytes={bytes} epoch={epoch} \
+                 segments={segments} files={}",
+                files_in(&self.directory)
+            );
+        }
+        Ok(format!("sealed={count}"))
+    }
+
+    /// One merge, with the store's own policy deciding what to take.
+    fn merge(&mut self) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        let plan = self.live()?.plan();
+        if plan.is_empty() {
+            return Err(
+                "the store's policy has nothing to merge, so there is no merge to watch: a \
+                        scenario that asks for one anyway is a scenario that is not measuring \
+                        anything"
+                    .into(),
+            );
+        }
+        let report = self
+            .live()?
+            .compact()?
+            .ok_or("a plan that is not empty is a merge")?;
+        self.merges += 1;
+        self.phase += 1;
+        println!(
+            "lakelet-sim merge epoch={} into={} taken={} bytes_in={} bytes_out={} removed={} \
+             records={} segments={} files={}",
+            report.epoch(),
+            report.wrote(),
+            list(report.merged()),
+            report.bytes_in(),
+            report.bytes_out(),
+            report.bytes_removed(),
+            report.records(),
+            report.segments_after(),
+            files_in(&self.directory)
+        );
+        self.answers_the_notebook()?;
+        Ok(format!("merged into={}", report.wrote()))
+    }
+
+    /// Kill the store at one stage of a merge and leave the disk where that stage leaves it.
+    fn crash(&mut self, stage: &str) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        if !STAGES.contains(&stage) {
+            return Err(format!("`{stage}` is not a stage a merge has").into());
+        }
+        let before = self.live()?.manifest().epoch();
+        let plan = self.live()?.plan();
+        if plan.is_empty() {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "stage {stage}: the store's policy has nothing to merge, so there is no merge to \
+                 interrupt"
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        let mut written = None;
+        match stage {
+            // The plan is a value and nothing else happened: no file was touched, and the store on
+            // the disk is the store it was.
+            "planned" => {}
+            "written" => {
+                let ran = self.live()?.merge(&plan)?;
+                written = Some(ran.index());
+                // The device committed part of the write and not the rest, which is the disk a kill
+                // inside a write leaves. Nothing reads it: the manifest does not name it.
+                let cut = std::fs::metadata(ran.file())
+                    .map_err(|error| format!("the new segment is a file: {error}"))?
+                    .len()
+                    / 2;
+                let file = std::fs::OpenOptions::new()
+                    .write(true)
+                    .open(ran.file())
+                    .map_err(|error| format!("the new segment opens for the cut: {error}"))?;
+                file.set_len(cut)
+                    .map_err(|error| format!("the write is cut short: {error}"))?;
+                file.sync_all()
+                    .map_err(|error| format!("the cut reaches the device: {error}"))?;
+            }
+            "durable" => {
+                let ran = self.live()?.merge(&plan)?;
+                written = Some(ran.index());
+            }
+            "swapped" | "retired" => {
+                // A reader is holding the old set: it is what keeps the segments a merge replaced on
+                // the disk, and leaking it below is what makes that a *death* rather than a clean
+                // stop.
+                let lingering = self.live()?.snapshot();
+                let ran = self.live()?.merge(&plan)?;
+                written = Some(ran.index());
+                self.live()?.install(ran)?;
+                self.merges += 1;
+                if stage == "swapped" {
+                    std::mem::forget(lingering);
+                } else {
+                    drop(lingering);
+                }
+            }
+            _ => {}
+        }
+        // Nothing is put back. What a death takes is the part of a write the device never
+        // committed, and the manifest is exactly what the stages before the swap have not touched:
+        // a merge that made itself visible while it wrote its segment dies here instead of being
+        // tidied up by this harness, which is the difference between modelling a crash and
+        // describing one.
+        self.deaths += 1;
+        let swapped = matches!(stage, "swapped" | "retired");
+        self.world = if swapped { before + 1 } else { before };
+        self.world_name = if swapped { "new" } else { "old" };
+        self.stage = stage.to_owned();
+        let epoch = self.live()?.manifest().epoch();
+        println!(
+            "lakelet-sim crash stage={stage} index={} epoch={epoch} files={}",
+            written.unwrap_or(0),
+            files_in(&self.directory)
+        );
+        Ok(format!("crashed stage={stage}"))
+    }
+
+    /// Bring the store back after a death, and check which world it is.
+    ///
+    /// The store and every snapshot are *leaked* rather than dropped: a process death runs no
+    /// destructors, and a store that tidied up its replaced segments on the way out would be a
+    /// clean stop wearing a crash's name. What the next open finds is therefore what the death left.
+    fn restart(&mut self) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        if let Some(store) = self.store.take() {
+            std::mem::forget(store);
+        }
+        for (_, held) in self.held.drain() {
+            std::mem::forget(held.snapshot);
+        }
+        let store = Segments::open(&self.directory)?;
+        let (epoch, segments, records) = {
+            let manifest = store.manifest();
+            (
+                manifest.epoch(),
+                manifest.segments().len(),
+                manifest.records(),
+            )
+        };
+        self.store = Some(store);
+        let answered = self.answers_the_notebook()?;
+        let files = files_in(&self.directory);
+        if files != segments as u64 {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "stage {}: the directory holds {files} file(s) and the manifest names {segments} — \
+                 opening a store is what finishes the merge a crash interrupted, and what is left \
+                 behind is a segment nobody installed or a segment a merge replaced, neither of \
+                 which the manifest names",
+                self.stage
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        if epoch != self.world {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "stage {}: the store came back at epoch {epoch} and this stage leaves epoch {} — a \
+                 crash in the middle of a merge leaves the old set of segments or the new one, and \
+                 the epoch is which of the two it is",
+                self.stage, self.world
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        println!(
+            "lakelet-sim restart stage={} world={} epoch={epoch} segments={segments} records={records} \
+             answered={answered} files={files}",
+            self.stage, self.world_name
+        );
+        Ok(format!("restarted epoch={epoch}"))
+    }
+
+    /// A reader takes a snapshot and holds it.
+    fn begin(&mut self, reader: &str) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        if self.held.contains_key(reader) {
+            return Err(format!("reader {reader} is already holding a snapshot").into());
+        }
+        let snapshot = self.live()?.snapshot();
+        let records = snapshot.len();
+        let epoch = snapshot.epoch();
+        let segments = snapshot.segments().len();
+        self.held
+            .insert(reader.to_owned(), Held { snapshot, records });
+        println!(
+            "lakelet-sim begin reader={reader} epoch={epoch} segments={segments} records={records}"
+        );
+        Ok(format!("holding epoch={epoch}"))
+    }
+
+    /// A reader reads what it holds, checks it against the notebook, and lets go.
+    fn end(&mut self, reader: &str) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        let Some(held) = self.held.remove(reader) else {
+            return Err(format!("reader {reader} is holding no snapshot to end").into());
+        };
+        let read = held.snapshot.records()?;
+        if read.len() as u64 != held.records {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "reader {reader}: the snapshot named {} record(s) and answered {} — the manifest is \
+                 the store's index, and a reader that answers a different number than it named is \
+                 reading a store nobody wrote",
+                held.records,
+                read.len()
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        if read.len() > self.notebook.len() || !same(&read, &self.notebook[..read.len()]) {
+            return Err(format!(
+                "reader {reader}: the snapshot answered {} record(s) and the notebook holds {} — \
+                 what a reader gets is the prefix of the store it named, record for record",
+                read.len(),
+                self.notebook.len()
+            )
+            .into());
+        }
+        let epoch = held.snapshot.epoch();
+        let segments = held.snapshot.segments().len();
+        let digest = digest(&read);
+        // The snapshot is dropped by the line above, so the file count here is what the store
+        // holds once this reader has let go of what it named — the number the deferral is about.
+        println!(
+            "lakelet-sim read reader={reader} epoch={epoch} segments={segments} records={} \
+             digest={digest:016x} files={}",
+            read.len(),
+            files_in(&self.directory)
+        );
+        // The claim this step is about, as a number: two readers that named the same store answer
+        // the same thing, whether a merge happened between them or not.
+        match self.answers.get(&epoch) {
+            Some(before) if *before != digest => {
+                return Err(format!(
+                    "reader {reader}: epoch {epoch} answered {digest:016x} and a reader that ran \
+                     entirely before it answered {before:016x} — a read that began before the swap \
+                     answers exactly what a read that ran entirely before the swap answered, and \
+                     the same records six ways is not the same store",
+                )
+                .into());
+            }
+            Some(before) => println!(
+                "lakelet-sim same epoch={epoch} digest={before:016x} reads={}",
+                self.reads + 1
+            ),
+            None => {
+                self.answers.insert(epoch, digest);
+            }
+        }
+        self.reads += 1;
+        Ok(format!("read records={}", read.len()))
+    }
+}
+
+/// One connection to the host, one command per line.
+struct Client {
+    stream: BufReader<TcpStream>,
+}
+
+impl Client {
+    /// Connect, waiting for the host to be listening.
+    async fn connect() -> Result<Self, Failure> {
+        for _ in 0..200 {
+            match TcpStream::connect((HOST, PORT)).await {
+                Ok(socket) => {
+                    return Ok(Self {
+                        stream: BufReader::new(socket),
+                    });
+                }
+                Err(_) => tokio::time::sleep(POLL).await,
+            }
+        }
+        Err(format!("{HOST}:{PORT} never accepted a connection").into())
+    }
+
+    /// Send one command and answer the reply. A command the host refused fails the run.
+    async fn ask(&mut self, command: &str) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+        self.stream.write_all(command.as_bytes()).await?;
+        self.stream.write_all(b"\n").await?;
+        self.stream.flush().await?;
+        let mut text = String::new();
+        if self.stream.read_line(&mut text).await? == 0 {
+            return Err(format!("the host closed the connection at `{command}`").into());
+        }
+        let reply = text.trim_end().to_owned();
+        match reply.strip_prefix("failed ") {
+            Some(reason) => Err(format!("`{command}` was refused: {reason}").into()),
+            None => Ok(reply),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Poll the host's status until the reply carries `needle`, or fail after `tries` looks.
+///
+/// Polling a condition instead of sleeping for a guessed duration is the rule every fixture in this
+/// course follows, and the bound is the other half of it: a scenario that waits for ever reports
+/// nothing, and the gate would call it a timeout instead of a run that never got there.
+async fn wait_for(client: &mut Client, needle: &str, tries: usize) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+    for _ in 0..tries {
+        let reply = client.ask("status").await?;
+        if reply.contains(needle) {
+            return Ok(reply);
+        }
+        tokio::time::sleep(POLL).await;
+    }
+    Err(format!("the host never said `{needle}`").into())
+}
+
+/// The host's own view, which is what the clients poll to stay in step.
+fn status(host: &Host) -> String {
+    match &host.store {
+        Some(store) => {
+            let manifest = store.manifest();
+            format!(
+                "status phase={} clients={} sealed={} epoch={} segments={} records={} held={} \
+                 merges={} deaths={} reads={} finished={}",
+                host.phase,
+                host.clients,
+                host.sealed,
+                manifest.epoch(),
+                manifest.segments().len(),
+                manifest.records(),
+                host.held.len(),
+                host.merges,
+                host.deaths,
+                host.reads,
+                host.finished
+            )
+        }
+        None => format!(
+            "status phase={} clients={} sealed={} down deaths={} reads={} finished={}",
+            host.phase, host.clients, host.sealed, host.deaths, host.reads, host.finished
+        ),
+    }
+}
+
+/// Read one command and answer the host's reply.
+async fn serve(socket: TcpStream, host: Arc<Mutex<Host>>) -> Result<(), Failure> {
+    {
+        host.lock().await.clients += 1;
+    }
+    let mut stream = BufReader::new(socket);
+    loop {
+        let mut text = String::new();
+        if stream.read_line(&mut text).await? == 0 {
+            return Ok(());
+        }
+        let reply = match command(text.trim(), &host).await {
+            Ok(reply) => reply,
+            Err(error) => format!("failed {error}"),
+        };
+        stream.write_all(reply.as_bytes()).await?;
+        stream.write_all(b"\n").await?;
+        stream.flush().await?;
+    }
+}
+
+/// One command, the one line it answers, and the work behind it.
+async fn command(text: &str, host: &Arc<Mutex<Host>>) -> Result<String, Failure> {
+    let mut words = text.split_whitespace();
+    let verb = words.next().unwrap_or_default();
+    let mut host = host.lock().await;
+    if let Some(reason) = host.failed.clone() {
+        return Err(reason.into());
+    }
+    match verb {
+        "status" => Ok(status(&host)),
+        "seal" => {
+            let count = number(words.next(), "count")?;
+            host.seal(count)
+        }
+        "merge" => host.merge(),
+        "crash" => {
+            let stage = words.next().unwrap_or_default().to_owned();
+            host.crash(&stage)
+        }
+        "restart" => host.restart(),
+        "begin" => {
+            let reader = words.next().unwrap_or_default().to_owned();
+            host.begin(&reader)
+        }
+        "end" => {
+            let reader = words.next().unwrap_or_default().to_owned();
+            host.end(&reader)
+        }
+        "done" => {
+            host.finished += 1;
+            Ok(format!("finished={}", host.finished))
+        }
+        "verify" => {
+            let answered = host.answers_the_notebook()?;
+            let (epoch, segments) = {
+                let manifest = host.live()?.manifest();
+                (manifest.epoch(), manifest.segments().len())
+            };
+            let digest = digest(&host.notebook);
+            println!(
+                "lakelet-sim verify epoch={epoch} segments={segments} records={answered} \
+                 digest={digest:016x} reads={} merges={} deaths={}",
+                host.reads, host.merges, host.deaths
+            );
+            Ok(format!("verified records={answered}"))
+        }
+        other => Err(format!("`{other}` is not a command this store speaks").into()),
+    }
+}
+
+/// One reader: it takes a snapshot, reads it, and lets go — `ROUNDS` times.
+///
+/// The first reader holds its first snapshot until a merge has happened beside it (`phase=1`), which
+/// is the schedule this step is judged on: a read that began before the swap and ends after it. Every
+/// other read is whatever the seed makes of two readers racing one writer.
+async fn read(name: &str, crossing: bool) -> Result<(), Failure> {
+    let mut client = Client::connect().await?;
+    // The store before the first seal is a real store with nothing in it, and reading it would be
+    // six lines about an empty list: a reader starts when there is something to read.
+    wait_for(&mut client, &format!("sealed={SEGMENTS}"), 4_000).await?;
+    for round in 0..ROUNDS {
+        client.ask(&format!("begin {name}")).await?;
+        if crossing && round == 0 {
+            wait_for(&mut client, "phase=1", 4_000).await?;
+        } else {
+            // One turn of the scheduler: the other reader and the controller are running beside
+            // this one, and which of them gets there first is the seed's business.
+            client.ask("status").await?;
+        }
+        client.ask(&format!("end {name}")).await?;
+    }
+    client.ask(&format!("done {name}")).await?;
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+/// The controller: build the store, merge while a reader is inside a read, and walk the deaths.
+async fn control() -> Result<(), Failure> {
+    let mut client = Client::connect().await?;
+    // Every client before the first seal: a merge judged against a storm that had not started yet
+    // would say something about the schedule that is not true of the schedule.
+    wait_for(
+        &mut client,
+        &format!("clients={}", READERS.len() + 1),
+        2_000,
+    )
+    .await?;
+
+    client.ask(&format!("seal {SEGMENTS}")).await?;
+    // The first merge waits for a reader to be holding a snapshot: that is the read this step is
+    // about, and it is the one the controller can guarantee rather than hope for.
+    wait_for(&mut client, "held=1", 4_000).await?;
+    client.ask("merge").await?;
+    // The readers finish before the deaths start: a process that dies takes its readers with it, and
+    // a reader still holding a snapshot when the store is reopened would be measuring this harness
+    // rather than the store.
+    wait_for(&mut client, &format!("finished={}", READERS.len()), 4_000).await?;
+
+    for stage in STAGES {
+        client.ask(&format!("seal {MORE}")).await?;
+        client.ask(&format!("crash {stage}")).await?;
+        client.ask("restart").await?;
+    }
+    client.ask("verify").await?;
+    Ok(())
+}
+
+#[test]
+fn a_merge_is_a_schedule_and_a_reader_never_sees_half_of_one() {
+    let seed = seed();
+    let directory = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(
+        "lakelet-sim-compaction-{}-{seed}",
+        std::process::id()
+    ));
+    let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&directory);
+
+    let host = Arc::new(Mutex::new(
+        Host::open(directory.clone()).expect("a directory this process owns is a store"),
+    ));
+
+    let mut builder = Builder::new();
+    builder.rng_seed(seed).enable_random_order();
+    let mut sim = builder.build();
+
+    let hosted = Arc::clone(&host);
+    sim.host(HOST, move || {
+        let host = Arc::clone(&hosted);
+        async move {
+            let listener = TcpListener::bind(("0.0.0.0", PORT)).await?;
+            loop {
+                let (socket, _) = listener.accept().await?;
+                let host = Arc::clone(&host);
+                tokio::spawn(async move {
+                    if let Err(error) = serve(socket, host).await {
+                        eprintln!("lakelet-sim a connection ended: {error}");
+                    }
+                });
+            }
+            #[allow(unreachable_code)]
+            Ok(())
+        }
+    });
+
+    // The seed is deliberately *not* in the transcript: the gate compares transcripts, and a
+    // transcript whose first line spells the seed would make two seeds look different without any
+    // event having differed. What tells the seeds apart here is the order of the lines below.
+    println!(
+        "lakelet-sim started segments={SEGMENTS} records={} readers={}",
+        SEGMENTS * PER_SEGMENT,
+        READERS.len()
+    );
+    for (number, reader) in READERS.iter().enumerate() {
+        let name = (*reader).to_owned();
+        sim.client(name.clone(), async move { read(&name, number == 0).await });
+    }
+    sim.client("controller", async move { control().await });
+
+    sim.run()
+        .unwrap_or_else(|error| panic!("the simulation did not finish: {error}"));
+    let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&directory);
+}
